IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `poa-dates`
IMPORT `poa-core`
IMPORT `poa-seat`

-- Powers of Attorney Act 1998 (Qld) -- cases from the seat of the claimant.
--
-- Every expected value is worked by hand from the deposited text, with the arithmetic in the comment above it; none was
-- copied from the encoding's output. The regulative rules are run with EVALTRACE where the trace ends FULFILLED, and
-- #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line where it ends in a breach. Clock: days.
--
-- Readings used (poa-dates.l4): "within 6 months after" a day ends on the same day 6 months later (or the last day of a
-- shorter month); "within 9 months", "3 months" and "1 year" likewise; "not earlier than 6 months after" a death: the
-- first day allowed is the same day 6 months later; "within 28 days after" ends 28 days later.
--
-- The story: Margaret, of Toowoomba, made an enduring power of attorney on 3 May 2021 appointing her son Daniel for
-- financial matters, with no time specified. Her will left her house to her granddaughter Ruby. In 2025 Daniel bought
-- Margaret's car from her and sold her house. Margaret died on 14 August 2026. HYPOTHETICAL facts throughout.
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

`d` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY

`EPA` MEANS `an attorney under an enduring power of attorney`
`general` MEANS `an attorney under a general power of attorney made under this Act`
`AHD` MEANS `an attorney under an advance health directive`
`SHA` MEANS `a statutory health attorney`
`other law` MEANS `an attorney under a power of attorney made otherwise than under this Act`
`a date not used` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2000

§ `Date arithmetic used below`

-- 14 August 2026 to 14 February 2027: Aug 14 -> Sep 14 is 31 days, -> Oct 14 30, -> Nov 14 31, -> Dec 14 30, -> Jan 14
-- 31, -> Feb 14 31: 184 days. To 14 May 2027: + 28 (Feb) + 31 (Mar) + 30 (Apr) = 273 days.
#ASSERT `the days from` (`d` 14 8 2026) `to` (`d` 14 2 2027) EQUALS 184
#ASSERT `the days from` (`d` 14 8 2026) `to` (`d` 14 5 2027) EQUALS 273
-- 31 August 2025 + 6 months: 31 February 2026 does not exist; 2026 is a common year: 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `the day` 6 `months after` (`d` 31 8 2025) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2026
-- 29 February 2024 + 12 months: 29 February 2025 does not exist: 28 February 2025.
#ASSERT `the day` 12 `months after` (`d` 29 2 2024) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2025

§ `s 33 -- could the attorney act`

-- Margaret's EPA specified no time: Daniel's financial power was exercisable from 3 May 2021, capacity or not (s 33(2)).
#ASSERT `Could my attorney exercise the power at the time` `a financial matter` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: no time was specified, so power for financial matters became exercisable once the enduring power of attorney was made (s 33(2))`
-- A time specified ("when two doctors certify me") that has come.
#ASSERT `Could my attorney exercise the power at the time` `a financial matter` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the time, circumstance or occasion the enduring power of attorney specifies has come (s 33(1))`
-- Specified, not come, but Margaret could not communicate decisions: impaired capacity (schedule 3, one limb fails).
#ASSERT `Could my attorney exercise the power at the time` `a financial matter` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the principal has impaired capacity for the matter, and the power is exercisable during that period (s 33(3))`
-- Specified, not come, full capacity: the Act gives no answer in its own words (fork line 1).
#ASSERT `Could my attorney exercise the power at the time` `a financial matter` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the Act does not make the power exercisable yet: the specified time, circumstance or occasion has not come and the principal has capacity (s 33(1))`
-- Personal matter (where Margaret lives) while she had capacity: "and not otherwise" (s 33(4)).
#ASSERT `Could my attorney exercise the power at the time` `a personal matter` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: power for a personal matter is exercisable only while the principal has impaired capacity for it (s 33(4))`
-- Personal matter when she could not understand decisions about it.
#ASSERT `Could my attorney exercise the power at the time` `a personal matter` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: power for a personal matter is exercisable while the principal has impaired capacity for it (s 33(4))`

§ `ss 41, 113-114 -- invalid documents`

-- An EPA made when Margaret understood its effect but was pressed by Daniel to sign: not freely and voluntarily (s 41(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `Can the court declare my power of attorney invalid` `EPA` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied the principal did not have the capacity necessary to make it (s 113(2)(a)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
-- Did not understand its nature and effect (s 41(1)(b)); the general-document capacity fact is ignored for an EPA.
#ASSERT `Can the court declare my power of attorney invalid` `EPA` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied the principal did not have the capacity necessary to make it (s 113(2)(a)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
-- A general power of attorney: s 41 does not apply; the general capacity fact does.
#ASSERT `Can the court declare my power of attorney invalid` `general` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 113(2) gives no ground for declaring it invalid on these facts`
#ASSERT `Can the court declare my power of attorney invalid` `general` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied the principal did not have the capacity necessary to make it (s 113(2)(a)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
-- Not properly witnessed (s 113(2)(b)).
#ASSERT `Can the court declare my power of attorney invalid` `EPA` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied it does not comply with the Act's other requirements (s 113(2)(b)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
-- Induced by undue influence (s 113(2)(c)).
#ASSERT `Can the court declare my power of attorney invalid` `EPA` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the court may declare it invalid if satisfied it is invalid for another reason, such as dishonesty or undue influence (s 113(2)(c)); if it does, it is void from the start (s 114) and the court may appoint attorneys (s 113(3))`
#ASSERT `Can the court declare my power of attorney invalid` `EPA` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 113(2) gives no ground for declaring it invalid on these facts`

§ `ss 96, 98, 99 -- invalid powers`

`revoked` MEANS `the document has been revoked, wholly or to the extent it gives the power`
`valid` MEANS `none: the power was valid when it was used`

-- Margaret revoked the EPA in writing; Daniel, not told, kept paying her bills from her account: no liability because of
-- the invalidity (s 98(2)).
#ASSERT `Is my attorney liable for acting under an invalid power` `EPA` `revoked` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an attorney who used the power without knowing it was invalid incurs no liability, to the principal or anyone else, because of the invalidity (s 98(2)); liability for anything else is not affected`
-- He knew of the revocation event (each limb of "know" in s 96 alone is enough).
#ASSERT `Is my attorney liable for acting under an invalid power` `EPA` `revoked` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 98 gives the attorney no protection on these facts: the attorney knew of the invalidity, knew of an event that invalidated the power, or had reason to believe it was invalid`
#ASSERT `Is my attorney liable for acting under an invalid power` `EPA` `revoked` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 98 gives the attorney no protection on these facts: the attorney knew of the invalidity, knew of an event that invalidated the power, or had reason to believe it was invalid`
#ASSERT `Is my attorney liable for acting under an invalid power` `EPA` `revoked` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 98 gives the attorney no protection on these facts: the attorney knew of the invalidity, knew of an event that invalidated the power, or had reason to believe it was invalid`
-- A power used before it was exercisable is an invalidity (s 96(b)(iii)).
#ASSERT `Is my attorney liable for acting under an invalid power` `general` `the power was not exercisable when it was used` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an attorney who used the power without knowing it was invalid incurs no liability, to the principal or anyone else, because of the invalidity (s 98(2)); liability for anything else is not affected`
#ASSERT `Is my attorney liable for acting under an invalid power` `EPA` `valid` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 98 does not arise: the power was valid when it was used`
#ASSERT `Is my attorney liable for acting under an invalid power` `SHA` `revoked` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the Act does not settle this: s 98 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 98(1))`

-- s 99(2): the buyer of Margaret's shares under the revoked power did not know: valid in the buyer's favour.
#ASSERT `Is the transaction valid in favour of the other person` `revoked` FALSE EQUALS `the transaction is, in favour of the third person, as valid as if the power were not invalid (s 99(2))`
#ASSERT `Is the transaction valid in favour of the other person` `revoked` TRUE EQUALS `no: s 99(2) does not protect a third person who knew of the invalidity`
#ASSERT `Is the transaction valid in favour of the other person` `valid` TRUE EQUALS `s 99(2) does not arise: the power was valid when it was used`

-- s 99(3)(a): purchase completed 30 November 2025; 3 months later is 30 February 2026, which does not exist: 28 February
-- 2026 is the last day. A declaration on 28 February 2026 is in time; on 1 March 2026 it is not. The transaction was
-- completed on 1 September 2025, more than a year after the EPA of 3 May 2021, so (b) does not help.
#ASSERT `Is it presumed the other person did not know of the invalidity` TRUE (`d` 28 2 2026) (`d` 30 11 2025) (`d` 1 9 2025) (`d` 3 5 2021) EQUALS `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: they made a statutory declaration before or within 3 months after the purchase was completed (s 99(3)(a))`
#ASSERT `Is it presumed the other person did not know of the invalidity` TRUE (`d` 1 3 2026) (`d` 30 11 2025) (`d` 1 9 2025) (`d` 3 5 2021) EQUALS `no: s 99(3) raises no conclusive presumption on these facts; whether the third person knew is a question of fact`
-- A declaration made before completion counts ("before or within").
#ASSERT `Is it presumed the other person did not know of the invalidity` TRUE (`d` 1 10 2025) (`d` 30 11 2025) (`d` 1 9 2025) (`d` 3 5 2021) EQUALS `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: they made a statutory declaration before or within 3 months after the purchase was completed (s 99(3)(a))`
-- s 99(3)(b): the transaction completed 3 May 2022, exactly 1 year after the EPA: in; 4 May 2022: out.
#ASSERT `Is it presumed the other person did not know of the invalidity` FALSE (`a date not used`) (`d` 30 11 2025) (`d` 3 5 2022) (`d` 3 5 2021) EQUALS `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: the transaction was completed within 1 year after the power of attorney was made (s 99(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `Is it presumed the other person did not know of the invalidity` FALSE (`a date not used`) (`d` 30 11 2025) (`d` 4 5 2022) (`d` 3 5 2021) EQUALS `no: s 99(3) raises no conclusive presumption on these facts; whether the third person knew is a question of fact`
-- A power made on 29 February 2024: the year ends on 28 February 2025.
#ASSERT `Is it presumed the other person did not know of the invalidity` FALSE (`a date not used`) (`d` 30 11 2025) (`d` 28 2 2025) (`d` 29 2 2024) EQUALS `it is conclusively presumed, in favour of the purchaser, that the third person did not know of the invalidity: the transaction was completed within 1 year after the power of attorney was made (s 99(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `Is it presumed the other person did not know of the invalidity` FALSE (`a date not used`) (`d` 30 11 2025) (`d` 1 3 2025) (`d` 29 2 2024) EQUALS `no: s 99(3) raises no conclusive presumption on these facts; whether the third person knew is a question of fact`

§ `s 73 -- conflict transactions`

`own` MEANS `the attorney's own interests`
`friend` MEANS `the interests of a relation, business associate or close friend of the attorney`
`nobody` MEANS `nobody has authorised it`
`unauthorised` MEANS `yes, a conflict transaction entered into without authorisation: the attorney has acted contrary to s 73(1) until it is authorised (s 73(5)); the court may still relieve the attorney (s 105)`
`after` MEANS `yes, a conflict transaction authorised afterwards: it is taken to be, and always to have been, as valid as if authorised beforehand (s 73(3) or s 118(4))`
`before` MEANS `yes, a conflict transaction, and the attorney was authorised to enter into it (s 73(1))`
`merely` MEANS `no: a transaction is not a conflict transaction merely because of that (s 73(7))`

-- Daniel bought Margaret's car (example 1), with no authorisation: contrary to s 73(1) until authorised (s 73(5)).
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `own` `something else` `nobody` EQUALS `unauthorised`
-- Lending Margaret's money to Daniel's close friend (example 2), authorised beforehand by Margaret.
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `friend` `something else` `the principal authorised it, its type, or conflict transactions generally, beforehand` EQUALS `before`
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `own` `something else` `the court authorised it beforehand under s 118(2)` EQUALS `before`
-- Authorised afterwards by Margaret while she had capacity, or by the court.
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `own` `something else` `the principal, having capacity, authorised it afterwards (s 73(2))` EQUALS `after`
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `another duty of the attorney` `something else` `the court authorised it afterwards (s 118(3))` EQUALS `after`
-- s 73(7): merely related; merely a possible beneficiary; joint property.
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `own` `merely that the attorney is related to the principal` `nobody` EQUALS `merely`
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `own` `merely that the attorney may be a beneficiary of the principal's estate` `nobody` EQUALS `merely`
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `own` `merely that the attorney, in their own right and for the principal, deals with or acquires a joint interest in property, or borrows or guarantees for that` `nobody` EQUALS `merely`
-- s 73(8) gift; example 6 dependants; no conflict at all.
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `own` `the transaction is a gift or donation made under s 88` `nobody` EQUALS `no: a gift or donation under s 88 is not a conflict transaction (s 73(8))`
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `friend` `the transaction provides for a dependant of the principal under s 89` `nobody` EQUALS `no: providing for a dependant under s 89 is not a conflict transaction (s 73(6), example 6)`
#ASSERT `Was my attorney allowed to enter into this transaction` `nothing: there is no possible conflict with the attorney's duty to the principal` `something else` `nobody` EQUALS `no: it is not a conflict transaction (s 73(6))`

-- s 73(9): the car dealer who bought from Daniel's business without knowing.
#ASSERT `Is the conflict transaction valid in favour of the other party` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the transaction is, in favour of the other party, as valid as if it were not a conflict transaction (s 73(9))`
#ASSERT `Is the conflict transaction valid in favour of the other party` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 73(9) does not protect the other party: they knew, or had reason to believe, it was a conflict transaction`
#ASSERT `Is the conflict transaction valid in favour of the other party` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 73(9) does not protect the other party: they knew, or had reason to believe, it was a conflict transaction`

§ `s 87 -- undue influence`

`presumed` MEANS `yes: the transaction gives rise to a presumption in your favour that you were induced to enter it by the attorney's undue influence (s 87)`
`not settled 87` MEANS `the Act does not settle this: s 87 raises the presumption only for an attorney under an enduring power of attorney or advance health directive, or their relation, business associate or close friend; the general law may still`

-- Margaret sold her car to Daniel, her EPA attorney.
#ASSERT `Is it presumed my attorney unduly influenced me` `EPA` `the attorney` EQUALS `presumed`
-- To Daniel's wife; to his household member; to his business associate; under an AHD.
#ASSERT `Is it presumed my attorney unduly influenced me` `EPA` `the attorney's spouse` EQUALS `presumed`
#ASSERT `Is it presumed my attorney unduly influenced me` `EPA` `a member of the attorney's household` EQUALS `presumed`
#ASSERT `Is it presumed my attorney unduly influenced me` `EPA` `a business associate of the attorney` EQUALS `presumed`
#ASSERT `Is it presumed my attorney unduly influenced me` `AHD` `a person with a close personal relationship with the attorney and a personal interest in the attorney's welfare` EQUALS `presumed`
-- To a stranger; and an attorney under a general power of attorney (not in s 87(a)).
#ASSERT `Is it presumed my attorney unduly influenced me` `EPA` `someone else` EQUALS `not settled 87`
#ASSERT `Is it presumed my attorney unduly influenced me` `general` `the attorney` EQUALS `not settled 87`

§ `ss 83-89 -- financial matters`


-- s 84: shares a trustee could hold; QCAT-approved; held already; a cryptocurrency punt Daniel made.
#ASSERT `Could my attorney invest my money this way` `EPA` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is an authorised investment (s 84(1))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney invest my money this way` `EPA` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is an authorised investment (s 84(1))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney invest my money this way` `EPA` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the attorney may continue an investment the principal had when the power became exercisable (s 84(2))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney invest my money this way` `EPA` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an attorney for financial matters may invest only in authorised investments (s 84(1))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney invest my money this way` `general` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

-- s 86: Daniel paid Margaret's pension into his own account.
#ASSERT `Did my attorney have to keep their property separate from mine` `EPA` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the attorney must keep their property separate from the principal's (s 86(1)); not doing so is an offence, up to 300 penalty units`
#ASSERT `Did my attorney have to keep their property separate from mine` `EPA` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 86(1) does not apply to property the principal and the attorney own jointly (s 86(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Did my attorney have to keep their property separate from mine` `EPA` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 86(1) does not apply to property they acquired jointly in place of jointly owned property (s 86(2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Did my attorney have to keep their property separate from mine` `other law` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

-- s 88: Christmas gifts to grandchildren as Margaret always gave; a $40,000 "gift" to Daniel she never would have made.
#ASSERT `Could my attorney give away my property` `EPA` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the gift meets s 88(1); the attorney, or a charity connected with them, may receive it (s 88(2))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney give away my property` `EPA` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the gift meets s 88(1); the attorney, or a charity connected with them, may receive it (s 88(2))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney give away my property` `EPA` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the gift is not of a nature the principal made when they had capacity, or might reasonably be expected to make (s 88(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney give away my property` `EPA` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the value is more than is reasonable given all the circumstances, in particular the principal's financial circumstances (s 88(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney give away my property` `EPA` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the gift is otherwise authorised under this Act (s 88(1))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney give away my property` `general` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

-- s 89: school fees for a grandchild living with Margaret and wholly dependent on her.
#ASSERT `Could my attorney provide for my dependant` `EPA` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the attorney may provide for the dependant's needs from the principal's estate, up to what is reasonable (s 89)`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney provide for my dependant` `EPA` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: what is provided must not be more than is reasonable given all the circumstances, in particular the principal's financial circumstances (s 89(2))`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney provide for my dependant` `EPA` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the enduring power of attorney expresses a contrary intention, so the reasonableness limit in s 89(2) does not apply`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney provide for my dependant` `EPA` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the Act does not settle this: s 89 is for a dependant, a person completely or mainly dependent on the principal`
#ASSERT `Could my attorney provide for my dependant` `AHD` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: Part 3 applies only to enduring powers of attorney (s 83)`

§ `Offences`

#ASSERT `What offence has my attorney committed` `not keeping the attorney's property separate from the principal's (s 86(1))` EQUALS `an offence; the most the court can impose is, in penalty units` 300
#ASSERT `What offence has my attorney committed` `not exercising power honestly and with reasonable diligence to protect the principal's interests (s 66(1))` EQUALS `an offence; the most the court can impose is, in penalty units` 200
#ASSERT `What offence has my attorney committed` `exercising a power the attorney knows has been revoked (s 71)` EQUALS `an offence; the most the court can impose is, in penalty units` 200
#ASSERT `What offence has my attorney committed` `dishonestly inducing a person to make or revoke an enduring document (s 61)` EQUALS `an offence; the most the court can impose is, in penalty units` 200
#ASSERT `What offence has my attorney committed` `dishonestly inducing a person to make or revoke a power of attorney (s 26)` EQUALS `an offence; the most the court can impose is, in penalty units` 200
#ASSERT `What offence has my attorney committed` `using confidential information gained as attorney other than as s 74 allows, without a reasonable excuse (s 74A)` EQUALS `an offence; the most the court can impose is, in penalty units` 200
#ASSERT `What offence has my attorney committed` `other conduct` EQUALS `the Act does not settle this: ss 26, 61, 66, 71, 74A and 86 do not make that conduct an offence`

§ `ss 66, 106 -- compensation`

-- Daniel sold Margaret's house to a friend at an undervalue (loss) and kept a commission (profit): either, not both; after
-- Margaret's death, to her estate.
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation or the profits from my attorney` `EPA` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to pay compensation for the loss or to account for the profits, but not both for the same exercise of power (s 106(1)-(2)), paid to` `the principal's estate`
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation or the profits from my attorney` `EPA` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to pay compensation for the loss (s 106(1)(a)), paid to` `the principal`
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation or the profits from my attorney` `general` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the court or tribunal may order the attorney to account for the profits (s 106(1)(b)), paid to` `the principal`
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation or the profits from my attorney` `EPA` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 106 gives no order on these facts: it needs a loss caused by the failure, or profits the attorney accrued from it`
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation or the profits from my attorney` `EPA` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 106 gives no order on these facts: it needs a failure to comply with this Act in the exercise of a power`
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation or the profits from my attorney` `SHA` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 106 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 106(9))`

-- s 106(5)-(6): both alive: no time in s 106; Margaret died 14 August 2026: 14 February 2027; Daniel died first on 31
-- August 2025 and Margaret later: 28 February 2026 (the first death, s 106(6)).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for compensation from the attorney` FALSE (`a date not used`) FALSE (`a date not used`) EQUALS `s 106 sets no application time while the principal and the attorney are both alive`
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for compensation from the attorney` TRUE (`d` 14 8 2026) FALSE (`a date not used`) EQUALS `apply within 6 months after the death (s 106(5)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` (`d` 14 2 2027)
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for compensation from the attorney` FALSE (`a date not used`) TRUE (`d` 31 8 2025) EQUALS `apply within 6 months after the death (s 106(5)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` (`d` 28 2 2026)
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for compensation from the attorney` TRUE (`d` 14 8 2026) TRUE (`d` 31 8 2025) EQUALS `apply within 6 months after the first death (s 106(6)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` (`d` 28 2 2026)
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for compensation from the attorney` TRUE (`d` 14 8 2026) TRUE (`d` 1 9 2026) EQUALS `apply within 6 months after the first death (s 106(6)); the court or tribunal may extend the time (s 106(7)); the last day is` (`d` 14 2 2027)

-- In time: the last day itself is in; the day after is out.
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time` TRUE (`d` 14 8 2026) FALSE (`a date not used`) (`d` 14 2 2027) EQUALS JUST (`in time; the last day is` (`d` 14 2 2027))
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time` TRUE (`d` 14 8 2026) FALSE (`a date not used`) (`d` 15 2 2027) EQUALS JUST (`out of time, unless the time is extended or the court directs otherwise; the last day was` (`d` 14 2 2027))
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time` TRUE (`d` 14 8 2026) TRUE (`d` 31 8 2025) (`d` 28 2 2026) EQUALS JUST (`in time; the last day is` (`d` 28 2 2026))
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time` TRUE (`d` 14 8 2026) TRUE (`d` 31 8 2025) (`d` 1 3 2026) EQUALS JUST (`out of time, unless the time is extended or the court directs otherwise; the last day was` (`d` 28 2 2026))
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time` FALSE (`a date not used`) FALSE (`a date not used`) (`d` 1 3 2026) EQUALS NOTHING

-- The rule itself: 184 days from 14 August 2026.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 106 -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the death` 184) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `apply for an order under s 106` 184)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the applicant, "s 106(5)-(6): the application was not made within 6 months after the death; ..." (day 185)
#TRACE `s 106 -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the death` 184 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `apply for an order under s 106` AT 185

§ `s 107 -- a lost benefit`

-- Ruby lost the house Margaret's will left her when Daniel sold it (and the proceeds were spent). Value $620,000.
#ASSERT `Can I apply for compensation for my lost benefit in the estate` `EPA` TRUE `the person whose benefit was lost (even if that person is the attorney)` 620000 EQUALS `you may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the principal's estate, which must not exceed the value of the lost benefit (s 107(2)-(3)); at most` 620000
#ASSERT `Can I apply for compensation for my lost benefit in the estate` `general` TRUE `the personal representative of the person whose benefit was lost` 620000 EQUALS `you may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the principal's estate, which must not exceed the value of the lost benefit (s 107(2)-(3)); at most` 620000
#ASSERT `Can I apply for compensation for my lost benefit in the estate` `EPA` TRUE `someone else` 620000 EQUALS `the Act does not give you a s 107 application: s 107(2) names the person whose benefit was lost, or their personal representative`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for compensation for my lost benefit in the estate` `EPA` FALSE `the person whose benefit was lost (even if that person is the attorney)` 620000 EQUALS `no: s 107 applies only if a benefit in the principal's estate, under the will, on intestacy or by another disposition taking effect on death, is lost because of a sale or other dealing with the principal's property by an attorney`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for compensation for my lost benefit in the estate` `SHA` TRUE `the person whose benefit was lost (even if that person is the attorney)` 620000 EQUALS `no: s 107 does not reach a statutory health attorney (s 107(5))`

-- 9 months after 14 August 2026 (Succession Act s 41(8), applied by s 107(4)): 14 May 2027.
#ASSERT `Is my lost benefit application in time` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 14 5 2027) EQUALS `in time; the last day is` (`d` 14 5 2027)
#ASSERT `Is my lost benefit application in time` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 15 5 2027) EQUALS `out of time, unless the time is extended or the court directs otherwise; the last day was` (`d` 14 5 2027)
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 107 -- the applicant must apply within 9 months after the principal's death` 273) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `apply for compensation under s 107` 273)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the applicant, "s 107(4) with Succession Act 1981 s 41(8): the proceedings were not started within 9 months ..." (day 274)
#TRACE `s 107 -- the applicant must apply within 9 months after the principal's death` 273 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `apply for compensation under s 107` AT 274

§ `ss 61A-61D -- proceeds`

-- Ruby, beneficiary of the house under Margaret's will; Daniel sold it under the EPA and $580,000 sits in Margaret's
-- account.
#ASSERT `Do I take the proceeds of property the attorney dealt with` `EPA` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you have the same interest in the proceeds as you would have had in the property, and you are entitled to income traced from them and any capital gain on them (s 61B(2)-(3)); this holds even if you are the attorney (s 61B(4))`
#ASSERT `Do I take the proceeds of property the attorney dealt with` `EPA` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes, but subject to the court's order under s 61D(1) (s 61B(5)): you have the same interest in the proceeds as you would have had in the property, with traced income and capital gains (s 61B(2)-(3))`
#ASSERT `Do I take the proceeds of property the attorney dealt with` `general` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: ss 61B to 61D apply only to an enduring power of attorney (s 61A)`
-- An heir on intestacy, not under a will.
#ASSERT `Do I take the proceeds of property the attorney dealt with` `EPA` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the Act does not settle this under s 61B: it is for a beneficiary under a deceased principal's will, of property an attorney dealt with under an enduring power of attorney (see s 107 for a lost benefit)`

`61D yes` MEANS `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court (not QCAT, s 61D(7)) for an order giving effect to s 61B, or preventing an unjust and disproportionate advantage or disadvantage the will did not contemplate (s 61D(1)-(2)); apply within 6 months after the principal's death (s 61D(4)), which the court may extend (s 61D(5))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the proceeds` `EPA` `a beneficiary under the principal's will` EQUALS `61D yes`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the proceeds` `EPA` `the personal representative of a deceased beneficiary under the principal's will` EQUALS `61D yes`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the proceeds` `EPA` `the personal representative of the principal` EQUALS `61D yes`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the proceeds` `EPA` `someone else` EQUALS `the Act does not give you a s 61D application: s 61D(2) names a beneficiary under the will, a deceased beneficiary's personal representative, and the principal's personal representative`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the proceeds` `other law` `a beneficiary under the principal's will` EQUALS `no: ss 61B to 61D apply only to an enduring power of attorney (s 61A)`

-- 6 months after 14 August 2026: 14 February 2027.
#ASSERT `Is my application about the proceeds in time` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 14 2 2027) EQUALS `in time; the last day is` (`d` 14 2 2027)
#ASSERT `Is my application about the proceeds in time` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 15 2 2027) EQUALS `out of time, unless the time is extended or the court directs otherwise; the last day was` (`d` 14 2 2027)
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 61D -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the principal's death` 184) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `apply for an order under s 61D` 184)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the applicant, "s 61D(4): the application was not made within 6 months after the principal's death; ..." (day 185)
#TRACE `s 61D -- the applicant must apply within 6 months after the principal's death` 184 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `apply for an order under s 61D` AT 185

§ `Succession Act s 44, applied -- the personal representative's distribution`

`no protection` MEANS `no: Succession Act s 44(2)-(3) does not protect this distribution`

-- No notice from Ruby: the first protected day is 14 February 2027 (6 months); 13 February 2027 is too early.
#ASSERT `Could the personal representative distribute the estate out of my reach` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 14 2 2027) TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for a distribution properly made at least 6 months after the death without notice of an application (Succession Act s 44(3)(a))`
#ASSERT `Could the personal representative distribute the estate out of my reach` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 13 2 2027) TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no protection`
-- Ruby gave written notice: 9 months, to 14 May 2027, unless the PR knew the application had started or was served.
#ASSERT `Could the personal representative distribute the estate out of my reach` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 14 5 2027) TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for a distribution properly made at least 9 months after the death, with no written notice that the application had been started and no service of it (Succession Act s 44(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `Could the personal representative distribute the estate out of my reach` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 13 5 2027) TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no protection`
#ASSERT `Could the personal representative distribute the estate out of my reach` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 14 5 2027) TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no protection`
-- Ruby consented in writing: protected at any time (s 44(2)); a distribution not properly made: never.
#ASSERT `Could the personal representative distribute the estate out of my reach` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `protected: no action lies against the personal representative for the distribution, because you notified them in writing that you consent to it or do not intend to apply (Succession Act s 44(2))`
#ASSERT `Could the personal representative distribute the estate out of my reach` (`d` 14 8 2026) (`d` 1 9 2027) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no protection`

§ `ss 109A, 110, 122-125 -- going to court`

`family` MEANS `yes, as a member of the principal's family (s 110(3)(b), (5))`
`not named` MEANS `the Act does not settle this: s 110 does not name you, so you may apply only as an interested person (someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests)`

-- Margaret's daughter Helen, 52; her brother Tom while Helen is available, and if no spouse, child or parent is.
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `the principal's child aged 18 or more (including a stepchild, adopted child, or a person the principal fostered or was guardian of as a child)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `family`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `the principal's sibling aged 18 or more (including a step-sibling, adopted sibling or foster-sibling)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not named`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `the principal's sibling aged 18 or more (including a step-sibling, adopted sibling or foster-sibling)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `family`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `the principal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as the principal (s 110(2), (3)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `the principal's spouse` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `family`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `the principal's parent (including a step-parent, adoptive parent, foster-parent or guardian)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `family`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `an attorney of the principal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as an attorney (s 110(3)(c))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `a health provider of the principal` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, as a health provider, because the application is about an advance health directive or a health matter (s 110(3)(e))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `a health provider of the principal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not named`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `another person with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, as an interested person: someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for the principal's rights and interests (s 110(2), (3)(f))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply to the court about the attorney` `another person` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not named`

`other chapter 6` MEANS `another declaration, order, direction, recommendation or advice under chapter 6`
#ASSERT `Which court or tribunal hears my application` `compensation or an account of profits under s 106` `general` EQUALS `any court, or QCAT (s 106(1), (9))`
#ASSERT `Which court or tribunal hears my application` `compensation for a lost benefit under s 107` `EPA` EQUALS `the Supreme Court (s 107(2))`
#ASSERT `Which court or tribunal hears my application` `an order under s 61D about a beneficiary's interest in proceeds` `EPA` EQUALS `the Supreme Court; not QCAT (s 61D(7))`
#ASSERT `Which court or tribunal hears my application` `an order for records, accounts or an audit under s 122` `EPA` EQUALS `the Supreme Court or QCAT (s 122(1))`
#ASSERT `Which court or tribunal hears my application` `other chapter 6` `EPA` EQUALS `the Supreme Court, or QCAT, because the document is an enduring document (ss 109A, 110)`
#ASSERT `Which court or tribunal hears my application` `other chapter 6` `AHD` EQUALS `the Supreme Court, or QCAT, because the document is an enduring document (ss 109A, 110)`
#ASSERT `Which court or tribunal hears my application` `other chapter 6` `general` EQUALS `the Supreme Court (s 110); QCAT has the court's jurisdiction only for enduring documents (s 109A)`

-- s 122: Helen, an interested person, wants Daniel's accounts, after Margaret's death (s 122(4)(b)).
#ASSERT `Can I get my attorney's accounts examined` `EPA` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the court or QCAT may order the attorney to file and serve on you a summary of receipts and expenditure, or more detailed accounts, for a stated period; have the accounts audited; or present a plan of management, even if the enduring power of attorney has been revoked or the principal has died (s 122)`
#ASSERT `Can I get my attorney's accounts examined` `EPA` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the Act does not settle this: s 122(2) names an application by the principal or another interested person (the court or QCAT may also act on its own initiative)`
#ASSERT `Can I get my attorney's accounts examined` `general` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the Act does not settle this: s 122 is for an attorney for a financial matter under an enduring power of attorney`
#ASSERT `Can I get my attorney's accounts examined` `EPA` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the Act does not settle this: s 122 is for an attorney for a financial matter under an enduring power of attorney`

-- ss 123, 125.
#ASSERT `Who pays the costs` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `costs follow the event: as the successful party you can expect your costs to be paid by the unsuccessful party (s 125(2))`
#ASSERT `Who pays the costs` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `costs follow the event: as the unsuccessful party you can expect to pay the successful party's costs (s 125(2))`
#ASSERT `Who pays the costs` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the court's order about costs governs (s 125)`
#ASSERT `Who pays the costs` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the court may order you to pay another participant's costs, and direct that you not make another application of that type without leave (s 123(2))`

-- s 124: reasons asked for on 1 December 2026: 28 days later is 29 December 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must the court give me written reasons` (`d` 1 12 2026) TRUE EQUALS `the court must give you written reasons by` (`d` 29 12 2026)
#ASSERT `By when must the court give me written reasons` (`d` 1 12 2026) FALSE EQUALS `no: s 124 obliges the court only on application by a person it considers has a sufficient interest in obtaining reasons`
#ASSERT EVALTRACE `s 124 -- the court must give written reasons within 28 days` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the court` `give written reasons for the decision` 28)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the court, "s 124: written reasons were not given within 28 days after the application for them" (day 29)
#TRACE `s 124 -- the court must give written reasons within 28 days` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the court` DOES `give written reasons for the decision` AT 29
